# The front door. One origin, routing by path — the same shape the ALB has in
# docs/INFRA.md's target architecture, which is the point of it being here rather than
# a convenience.
#
# It exists because **Next resolves `rewrites()` at build time**, into
# `.next/routes-manifest.json`. `next.config.ts` reads `API_ORIGIN` from the environment,
# so a container built on a laptop bakes `http://localhost:8000` and keeps it however the
# runtime environment is set — measured: `API_ORIGIN=http://api:8000` in the container,
# `localhost:8000` in the manifest, every proxied request `ECONNREFUSED`. Passing it as a
# build arg would fix that by making the image environment-specific, which is the opposite
# of what a container is for.
#
# So the web app does not proxy in the container at all. Its rewrites remain a `pnpm dev`
# convenience for the case where there is no front door.

:3000 {
	# Order matters: these three are the API's, everything else is the app's.
	# `/health` stays outside `/api/v1` deliberately (docs/API.md) — it is what a load
	# balancer polls, and routing it here is what the ALB will do too.
	handle /api/* {
		reverse_proxy api:8000
	}
	handle /auth/* {
		reverse_proxy api:8000
	}
	handle /health {
		reverse_proxy api:8000
	}
	handle {
		reverse_proxy web:3000
	}

	# ~~**Deliberately no `encode`.** Compression buys almost nothing on one machine and
	# risks the thing that matters most here: a proxy that buffers `text/event-stream`
	# turns a live interview transcript into one delivery at the end, which looks like a
	# hung model rather than a proxy setting.~~
	#
	# ~~(The first attempt did try to compress everything *except* SSE. Caddy 2.8 has no
	# `not` response matcher for `encode`, so it refused the config and restart-looped —
	# which is the better failure of the two available.)~~
	#
	# **Corrected 2026-09-21.** The risk was real and the conclusion was not. There is no
	# `not` response matcher, but there does not need to be: within one `match` block,
	# repeated values of the *same* header field are OR'd, so an allowlist of the content
	# types worth compressing says "not SSE" without saying `not`.
	#
	# The half of the reasoning that was simply wrong is "buys almost nothing". Next
	# compresses its own responses, so the app bundle was never the problem — but `/api/*`
	# goes to FastAPI, which mounts no middleware, so **every API response was going over
	# the wire raw**. Measured through this front door against the live stack:
	#
	#     /api/v1/concepts                     81,263 B -> 20,198 B   (-75%)
	#     /api/v1/jobs                         98,321 B -> 14,176 B   (-86%)
	#     /api/v1/practice/problems?limit=100  25,394 B ->  3,986 B   (-84%)
	#
	# That is ~200 KB of JSON on a dashboard-plus-jobs visit, and it is the deployed
	# topology this mirrors — an ALB and a browser that is not on this machine — where it
	# is felt, not `localhost`.
	#
	# **The SSE route is matched out by request path, and the content-type allowlist is
	# not enough on its own.** That was measured the hard way. With the allowlist alone an
	# event stream did come back with no `Content-Encoding`, and its frames did arrive one
	# at a time — and the response *header block* was still held until the first body
	# byte, because the encoder wraps the writer before it can know whether it will
	# encode. The API pings every 15s, so against the live stack:
	#
	#     no encode               headers at t+ 0.00s
	#     encode, allowlist only  headers at t+15.00s
	#
	# No event is lost and the transcript is fine, but `EventSource` does not fire
	# `onopen` until those headers land, so a live session would sit on "connecting" for
	# fifteen seconds at the start of every interview. The struck-out comment above was
	# right about the risk and wrong about the mechanism: the body is not buffered, the
	# header is.
	#
	# The allowlist stays as the second line of defence — it is what keeps any other
	# streaming or already-compressed response from being wrapped. `zstd` is first because
	# Caddy negotiates in order and it beats gzip where a client offers both.
	@compressible not path_regexp ^/api/v1/sessions/[^/]+/events$
	encode @compressible zstd gzip {
		match {
			header Content-Type application/json*
			header Content-Type application/problem+json*
			header Content-Type text/html*
			header Content-Type text/css*
			header Content-Type application/javascript*
			header Content-Type text/javascript*
			header Content-Type image/svg+xml*
		}
	}

	log {
		output stdout
		format console
	}
}
